The original [incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) calls this one known parser bug.
The exported reports and tenant directory show two independently fixable causes.
This review covers the 4 October export, not an assurance about the current queue.
Counts below are computed from the source reports, deduplicated by report ID.

<Callout>
Keep parser and booking-form hint failures in separate engineering tickets.
A shared symptom or matching subject is not proof of a shared cause.
The desk can keep receiving and merging reports after this snapshot.
</Callout>

## What each ticket covers

<CauseTable ticket={4471} {ticketLinks} />

### Parser: tenant day-first dates read month first

The shared parser treats date input as month first even when the tenant locale
is day first. For example, Harbour Dental (`en-GB`, `booking-v2`) reported an
April reminder sent in July in `R-41200`. This is not determined by the
reporter's browser locale or by `meta.intakeTag`.

Reproduce the represented failure by passing an unambiguous intended tenant date,
7 April 2026 entered as `07/04/2026`, through the month-first shared parser:
the result is 4 July, not 7 April. The product runtime is not in this export,
so this is a code-documented reproduction to verify in the product, not a test
we ran against production.

Fix the shared parsing contract to use the tenant's format, or transmit an
unambiguous date representation. Cover app/email paths and `booking-v2` form
paths. A `booking-v3` tenant's app report is still parser, not automatically hint.
Retain the one-day reports below as follow-up within this provisional grouping.

### Hint: booking-v3 instructs month first but reads tenant order

For day-first tenants using `booking-v3`, the form shows `MM/DD/YYYY`, but
interprets what people enter in the tenant's date order. Someone following
that hint for 7 April enters `04/07/2026`; a day-first form reads 4 July.
Only the form channel on that booking version belongs to this cause.

Affected form tenants are Alder Physio, Copper Studio and Pale Harbour.
Fix the hint and form input interpretation together. Correcting only the
shared parser does not resolve a misleading form instruction.

